course: english
lesson: 10
title: i before e, silent letters and other famous traps
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The old rhyme says «i before e except after c». It works well for the /ee/ sound: believe, field, piece, and after c, receive, ceiling, deceive. But it is a guide, not a law: «weird», «seize» and «protein» break it, and words like «their», «eight», «weigh» and «neighbour» do not have the /ee/ sound at all, so the rhyme was never about them. Many ei words say /ay/: eight, weigh, neighbour.
explain: Silent letters are fossils of old speech. The k in «knife» and «knee» and the w in «write» and «wrong» were once said aloud; so was the b in «lamb», «climb» and «thumb». The way people spoke changed, but the spelling stayed. «Wednesday» keeps its first d from «Woden's day», the day named after an old god, though most people say «Wenzday»; to spell it, say «Wed-nes-day» in your head.
explain: Not every silent letter used to be spoken. The s in «island» was never said: hundreds of years ago scholars added it because they wrongly thought the word came from the Latin «insula». A story like that is a great memory hook.
explain: Some words are traps even for grown-ups. «Friend» is an ie word that breaks the /ee/ rule, so learn it with a trick: a friend to the end. «February» has an r that many people skip in speech, so say «Feb-RU-ary» in your head when you write it. «Rhythm» has two h's and no ordinary vowel; «Rhythm Helps Your Two Hips Move» spells it out.
explain: The wise approach: use the full rhyme only for /ee/, learn silent letters in groups (kn, wr, mb, gn), and keep a short list of the famous oddballs.
fact: «i before e except after c» works mainly for the /ee/ sound: believe, field, receive, ceiling.
fact: After c, the /ee/ sound is usually spelled ei: receive, ceiling, deceive.
fact: Some words break the rhyme: weird, seize, protein.
fact: Many ei words make the /ay/ sound: eight, weigh, neighbour; the rhyme does not cover them.
fact: Silent letters often show how words were said long ago: the k in «knife» was once spoken.
fact: Common silent-letter groups: kn (knife, knee), wr (write, wrong), mb (lamb, climb, thumb), gn (gnome).
fact: The s in «island» was added by scholars who wrongly linked the word to Latin «insula»; it was never spoken.
fact: «Wednesday» comes from «Woden's day» and keeps a d most people do not say; saying «Wed-nes-day» in your head helps you spell it.
fact: «Friend» is an exception to the /ee/ rule; «a friend to the end» helps.
fact: «February» has an r that many people skip when they speak.
fact: «Rhythm» is spelled r-h-y-t-h-m, with two h's.
fact: «Neighbour» is the British spelling, with ei for /ay/ and an -our ending; American English writes «neighbor».
fact: Slang in this lesson: «wicked» means very in New England; «delulu» means delusional, far from reality; «innit» means «isn't it» or «right?».
exercise: Jack says «weird» breaks the rhyme. Is he right? | Yes, it is one of the exceptions | No, «weird» follows the rule | No, the c in «weird» explains it | Yes, so the rhyme is always wrong
exercise: Sophie writes «recieve». What is right? | receive | receeve | recive | reseive
exercise: Dad in Boston says the rule is «wicked dumb» because of «their». What does «wicked» mean there? | Very | Evil | Clever | Slightly
exercise: Why doesn't the rhyme fit «their»? | «Their» has no /ee/ sound | «Their» comes after a c | «Their» is a plural word | The rhyme is only for verbs
exercise: Which sound do the letters ei make in «eight» and «weigh»? | /ay/ | /ee/ | /ie/ | /e/
exercise: Ava writes «nife». What is missing, and why? | A silent k that was once said | A silent e, to make the i long | An extra f, because f always doubles | Nothing, «nife» is the new spelling
exercise: Leo writes «lam». What is right? | lamb | lambe | lamm | lame
exercise: Ruby asks why «island» has an s. What is the answer? | Scholars added it by mistake long ago | The s was always said, until recently | It shows the word is a plural | It is there to make it look like «sand»
exercise: Holly writes «Wensday». Where does the d come from? | Woden's day | Wet day | Wednes, an old number | A spelling mistake that stuck
exercise: Mark writes «freind». Which trick helps? | A friend to the end | Fry the end | i before e always | Free in the end, with ee in the middle
exercise: Noah calls «February» «delulu spelling». What does «delulu» mean? | Far from reality | Delicious | Very old and old-fashioned | Difficult
exercise: Which letter do many people skip when they say «February»? | the first r | the b | the y | the F
exercise: Beth writes «nieghbour». What is right? | neighbour | neihgbour | nieghbor | naybour
exercise: Chris writes «rythm». What is right? | rhythm | rhythym | rythym | rhytm
exercise: Which word has a silent w? | write | wet | wind | sweet
exercise: Which word has a silent g? | gnome | gate | bag | green
test: «i before e except after c, always, no exceptions, sorted». What's the problem? | It's a guide for /ee/; words like «weird» break it | Nothing, the rhyme has no exceptions | It is only for words that start with c, like «ceiling» and «cereal» | It should be «e before i except after c»
test: «ceiling, that's c-i-e, cos i before e innit». Put her right. | After c it's ei: c-e-i-l-i-n-g | She's right, c-i-e-l-i-n-g | It's spelled «seeling» | Both orders are fine
test: «that rule is wicked dumb, what about their?» What would a teacher say? | The rule is only for /ee/; «their» doesn't have that sound | Dad is right; the rule is useless for every single word in English | «Their» is spelled «thier» | «Their» follows the rule because it has no c
test: What would happen if we spelled every silent letter out of English? | Words would be easier to spell but would lose clues to their history | Nothing, silent letters do nothing | Every word would be read the same in every accent | Words like «knight» and «night» would still look different, because their meanings differ
test: «the s in island is cos they used to say iss-land back in the day». Is that true? | No, the s was never said; scholars added it by mistake | Yes, everyone said «iss-land» until 1900 | Yes, and Americans still say it that way | No, the s was added last year
test: «I'm writin' Wensday cos it's what I say, innit». What should she do? | Say «Wed-nes-day» in her head when she spells it | Write «Wensday», spelling always follows speech | Write «Wendsday», with the d at the end | Write «Weds» in all her school work, since that is the short form everyone uses
test: «Feb-ru-ary, who even says that r? just drop it». What is the fair answer? | Many don't say it, but the spelling keeps it | Drop it; spelling should follow speech | It is spelled «Febuary» now | Only Americans spell it with the r; in Britain it has been dropped from spelling
test: A cartoon knight shouts «My name starts with N!». What should the cartoon teach? | «Knight» starts with a silent k | The knight is right, it's «Night» | «Knight» starts with a silent g | Knights have no spelling
test: Mark's friend spells «friend» as «freind». Which memory trick fixes it? | A friend to the end: the word ends in «end» | i before e except after c | Friends eat ice: f-r-e-i | Write it as it sounds: frend
test: ★★★ Which word follows the FULL rhyme, «i before e except after c, when the sound is /ee/»? | receipt | weird | eight | their, because it has no c
test: ★★★ How many of these words have a silent letter: knee, lamb, wet, write, gnome, bed? | 4 | 3 | 5 | 6
test: ★★★ Which group shares one silent-letter pattern? | lamb, climb, comb | knife, write, gnome | island, Wednesday, rhythm | eight, weigh, weird
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the rule «i before e except after c»? | It is a reliable guide for words containing the sound /ee/, subject to exceptions | It applies without exception to every word in the language | It applies exclusively to words commencing with the letter c | It was abolished by the examination boards some decades ago and is no longer taught in any school of the kingdom
exam: Identify the principal origin of most silent letters in English words. | They represent sounds formerly pronounced, which the spelling has preserved | They were inserted by printers in order to fill the line | They were introduced in order to render spelling more difficult for pupils | They represent sounds that will be pronounced in future
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the letter s in «island». | It was inserted by scholars in the erroneous belief that the word derived from Latin | It has been pronounced throughout the history of the word | It indicates that the word is a plural noun | It was inserted in the present century by lexicographers in order to distinguish the word from «Iceland» and from «highland»
exam: Ascertain the sound represented by the letters ei in the word «neighbour». | The sound /ay/, as in the word «eight» | The sound /ee/, as in the word «receive» | The sound /ie/, as in the word «height» | No sound whatever, the letters being silent
